---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import GuideCta from '../components/GuideCta.astro';
import { SCALES, DEFAULT_HONORS, DEFAULT_AP } from '../scripts/gpa';
const LETTERS = ['A', 'A-', 'B+', 'B', 'B-', 'C+', 'C', 'C-', 'D+', 'D', 'F'];
const withBonus = (letter: string, bonus: number) => { const p = SCALES.plus[letter]; return (p > 0 ? p + bonus : 0).toFixed(1); };
const rows = LETTERS.map((l) => [l, withBonus(l, 0), withBonus(l, DEFAULT_HONORS), withBonus(l, DEFAULT_AP)]);
---
<PageLayout type="article" enOnly published="2026-10-23" modified="2026-11-03" title="Weighted GPA Scale: How Much AP & Honors Classes Add" description="A common weighted GPA scale adds +0.5 for Honors and +1.0 for AP, IB and Dual Enrollment. See what every grade is worth at each level, with examples." path="/ap-honors-gpa-weights/" heading="Weighted GPA Scale: How Much Do AP and Honors Classes Add?">
  <p>Weighted GPA rewards advanced coursework by adding bonus points. A common system is +0.5 for Honors and +1.0 for AP, IB and Dual Enrollment. Your school may differ, so always confirm in your course handbook.</p>

  <h2>Weighted GPA scale chart</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="tbl">
      <caption class="sr-only">Grade points for each letter grade by class level, on a plus/minus scale</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Grade</th><th scope="col">Regular</th><th scope="col">Honors (+0.5)</th><th scope="col">AP, IB, DE (+1.0)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td class="num">{r[1]}</td><td class="num">{r[2]}</td><td class="num">{r[3]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>An F earns no bonus in this chart. An A+ usually counts the same as an A on this scale.</p>

  <h2>Do Honors and AP classes boost your GPA?</h2>
  <p>They can raise your weighted GPA, but not your unweighted GPA. The size of the boost depends on how many classes you take. With six equal 1-credit classes, one AP class adds 1.0 ÷ 6 = 0.17 to your weighted GPA compared with earning the same grade in a Regular class.</p>

  <h2>Variations you may see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>+0.25 / +0.75:</strong> some districts use smaller bonuses.</li>
    <li><strong>Bonus only for passing grades:</strong> some schools add weight only to A, B and C grades.</li>
    <li><strong>Different scales:</strong> a few schools use a 6.0 or 100-point weighted scale.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixed rules:</strong> IB Standard Level, AP seminar or dual-credit classes may be weighted differently.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Is it worth taking harder classes?</h2>
  <p>A B in an AP class (4.0 weighted) equals an A in a Regular class in weighted GPA, and colleges value the rigor. But colleges also want strong grades, so choose a challenge you can handle alongside activities and sleep.</p>

  <h2>Match your school</h2>
  <p>In the <a href="/#calculator">GPA calculator</a>, pick each class level and edit the custom bonus to match your handbook. Then see the effect on your <a href="/weighted-vs-unweighted-gpa/">weighted and unweighted GPA</a>.</p>
  <GuideCta current="/ap-honors-gpa-weights/" />
</PageLayout>
